<Page
  title="About — Zonesteward"
  description="Built by one person running a fleet of Cloudflare zones for an agency, because the dashboard assumes you only have one."
  eyebrow="About"
  h1="Built by the person who needed it."
>
  <p class="dropcap">
    I run Cloudflare for an agency. Dozens of client sites, across several
    Cloudflare accounts, on a mix of Free and Pro plans, and a support queue
    that doesn't care which zone the problem is in. The dashboard is very good
    at one zone at a time, and I have never once had one zone at a time.
  </p>
  <p>
    So I built the thing I wanted: somewhere I could ask <em>"who is attacking
    us right now?"</em> and get an answer across every zone, see traffic on one
    globe instead of forty tabs, and change a WAF rule on a client's production
    site without the sick feeling that comes from having no preview and no way
    back.
  </p>
  <p>
    The part I'm proudest of is the part that says no. The model can only
    propose. It has no write tool to be talked out of. Every change goes
    through a human, gets checked against reality at execution time, gets read
    back from Cloudflare, and can be undone with a click. That's not a feature
    layered on afterwards — the architecture wouldn't work any other way.
  </p>
  <h2>Where it is</h2>
  <p>
    In production, running my own fleet daily, and being opened to a private
    beta of twenty agencies and MSPs who have the same problem. It is
    founder-built and founder-run. There is no team page because there is no
    team yet, and I'd rather tell you that than pretend.
  </p>
  <p>
    The <a href="/security">security page</a> lists what's finished and what
    isn't. The <a href="/blog">writing</a> is where I explain the decisions.
  </p>
  <h2>Get in touch</h2>
  <p>
    <a href="mailto:hello@zonesteward.com">hello@zonesteward.com</a>. I read all of it.
  </p>
  <p style="margin-top:44px"><a href="/apply" class="btn btn-primary">Apply for beta →</a></p>
</Page>
